  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 PM MDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThe trend still remains for whatever shower and thunderstorm\nactivity is out there through Sunday morning to remain across\nthe central mountains. We will have to watch late this afternoon\nand evening for the potential for development from Jackpot up\nthrough Hailey, which is a little farther south from where the\ncurrent forecast has things redeveloping. Even if we see that \ndevelopment, it quickly shifts north into the central mountains\nby late evening. Otherwise, looks for breezy southerly winds.\nGusts of 25-35 mph will be common through this evening,\nlingering across the southeast highlands up through the Teton\nValley with downsloping into the Snake Plain from Pocatello\nnorth. The main issues remain for tomorrow night and Labor Day -\nincreasing showers and thunderstorms followed by stronger winds\nlate in the day. We may catch a small amount of moisture from\nMarie closer to the Wyoming border, but amounts even with that\npossibility continue to drop off. The best chance of rainfall\nlooks more and more plausible over portions of the central\nmountains associated with low itself. Most areas now will only\nsee a few hundredths to around 0.30\", including in the\nmountains. We may see up 0.50\" across higher elevations of the\neastern highlands, with a 30-50% chance of exceeding that\namount. Areas north of Stanley and through the Frank Church are\nlooking at 0.40-0.70\" with the latest forecast, with a 40-60% \nchance of more than 0.75\". Showers and storm will clear most\nareas by afternoon except for the central mountains and along\nthe Montana border, and all areas by midnight. Winds ahead of\nshowers and storms tomorrow will be out of the south and\nsouthwest at 15-30 mph (sustained and gusts) with some spots\nseeing gusts around 35-40 mph. Adding in potential gusts with\nstorms, we could see outflow winds approaching 50-60 mph\ntomorrow afternoon and evening. There is a MARGINAL RISK for\nsevere storms over most of eastern Idaho for this reason. On\nLabor Day, winds will be west to southwest...strongest from the\nMagic Valley up into southwest Montana. Sustained 25-35 mph with\ngusts above 40 mph are possible, pushing WIND ADVISORY\nthresholds across the Arco Desert. There will be a very sharp\ncontrast tomorrow vs Monday with temperatures. We will 70s and\nlow 80s for highs in the valleys Sunday. Monday will be 10-15\ndegrees COOLER with only a few spots possibly getting above 70.\nIt will be cold enough Monday with the low crossing the central\nmountains to see a rain/snow mix or brief period of all snow for\nelevations above 10,000ft!  \n\nTuesday looks dry, and so does most of Wednesday. (We might see\na few random showers in the afternoon). There is a slightly\nhigher chance of showers and storms Thursday, but likely\nincreases steadily into next weekend. Temperatures rebound with\n70s and 80s quickly returning at the end of the upcoming week.",
